hello and welcome to the new world  review your selves for everything anime  and manga and today we're here to talk  about attack on Titan season 3 now in  classic new world review style yesterday  and late to this series not attack on  Titan but season 3 specifically and it's  because I have a very strange  relationship with attack on Titan  because I watched the first series when  it was airing loved it then tried the  manga and hated it because the art  really was not for me not bashing the  art just saying that it was something  that I never grew accustomed to and then  I just forgot about it because there  wasn't another enemy for what four years  when season 2 came out I watched it and  it was good yeah but I feel like it  really lost that zing that originally  caught me and as a result I took my  sweet time with season 3 but I have to  say that as a result I am incredibly  pleasantly surprised i sat down to watch  it thinking that it was just time to do  my duty as an anime fan and then I uh  well I just didn't stop season 3 really  managed to recapture some of that morbid  magic that the first anime outing had  and before we go any further this is  your spoiler warning I am more than  likely going to talk about very specific  events so bear that in mind but first up  the thing that struck me the most is  obviously the beautiful animation which  studio is a pretty damn incredible group  as they continue to demonstrate with the  ongoing Vinland soccer anime which I'm  also loving but in this case I am  convinced that they have elevated tackle  on Titan beyond anything the original  manga could have dreamed  once again that's just my opinion like  for example when I did try to read the  manga I got up to the end of the first  half of the season with Aaron being  captured again and all the historian of  and in the manga I vividly remember  being bored whilst reading this which is  why afterwards I would have dropped it  and that clearly isn't a fault of the  storytelling but the presentation  because season 3 is the anime takes the  very same material and manages to keep  me incredibly invested with stunning  aesthetic storytelling and in particular  I find myself captivated by pretty much  any action scene in season 3 but even in  the solo moments of which there are an  abundance especially in the first half  the art is just so slick and sleek and  it's hard to describe but it presents  such an incredibly solid image it's so  easy to watch and the anime takes  advantage of lighting as well crafting  beautiful sunsets terrifying darkness  and apocalyptic battle conditions and I  guess what I'm trying to say is that  every shot in this series  feels incredibly rich as opposed to a  more classic anime style which can be  very flat and you know that works for a  lot of shows but I am glad that we're  going a step further here in terms of  the visual  one negative I will say is that I'm  still not keen on the CGI whatsoever so  yes I'm one of those people like the  whole portion with the colossal Titan  wall amazing was a bit iffy in the  moments where CGI was used to bring it  to life it just looks super clunky not  natural at all and most importantly not  threatening because it looks like a  university animated students project and  yeah that does not apply universally  there were some sequences where it did  look alright but that was primarily due  to the camera work rather than the  actual CGI itself just drop the CGI it  does not belong in this world  and it always somewhat breaks my  immersion because it's such a stark  contrast to the beautiful 2d art and  animation on display otherwise and you  know what in a series of lesser 2d  quality yeah you could probably get away  with this kind of CGI but not attack on  Titan but hey at least it's not berserk  now I have to say that in retrospect the  thing I enjoyed most about season 3 was  finally getting some answers to the  story rather than just obtaining more  questions I mean yeah mysteries are the  spice of series like this but they can  only last so long and I'm thrilled that  we are finally getting into the meat of  things and some of the best and most  heartbreaking episodes of season 3 what  the ones have focused on krish's  memories and of course I give full  credit to the manga here because this  section of story was spectacular and it  gave me a whole new appreciation for  Titans that I never thought I'd be  capable of feeling I mean just watching  these episodes made me feel absolutely  enraged and it was a massive punch in  the gut which was assisted by fantastic  vocal performances some wonderful  Twilight lighting at the site of  paradise and unbelievably emotive music  as well as very intentional moments of  atmospheric silence that assists the  intimacy of these scenes a lot and also  just as a side note wow do I definitely  hate Zeke as a result so before all of  this I was pretty much indifferent to  his existence as the beast Titan and he  was pretty terrifying and kind of cool  but his flashback actually makes me  flashback  to the point in the season where Levi  had the chance to end him for good and I  understand why narrative Lee anyway he  couldn't die here because Zeke  ultimately holds some sort of importance  to Aaron as they came from the same  source of jeers as I guess but I do feel  like attack on Titan is just full of  these moments where victory is as simple  as one tiny action and then some sort of  ridiculous mental block just stops at  happening each time in fact the exact  same thing went on to happen with Reiner  when John prolonged his life and then  later broke down and yelled it's all my  fault  and in that moment I was just like yes  yes it is all your fault and I do get  the basic  idea was to steal their powers as well  as having some complex thought behind it  like Levi wanting to save Ellen and John  just having feelings for a former  comrade but for God's sake these Titans  just killed almost 200 Scouts and they  will inevitably cause such bloodshed  again the next time you meet them in  battle  so take the loss on the Titan powers and  end it here and that's actually why I  was so glad that Bertolt actually had a  conclusion because there was a while  there where it was taking an awfully  long time for things to happen and I  thought he may have been able to escape  as well but instead we got one of the  greatest moments of the series when he  was eaten by Titan Arman and it's weird  because yeah I did feel sorry for Bert  old but then I remembered the staggering  amount of people that he had mercilessly  killed and then you know everything was  okay I'm also really glad that it  happened because tackle on Titan has a  really weird character dynamic I find  that they're either generally so squishy  and expendable that they are literally  made to die or they're completely  immortal and can never be killed no  matter how bad a situation gets and an  example of characters like that would be  corny Oh Sasha I love both of them but  the only reason they survived this  season was because they are secondary  protagonists there were individuals far  more skilled and experienced who were  wiped out instantly and it does get a  bit plot armory to having to keep  believe that Wow of the nine survivors  they all happen to be our main cast  again except one which was actually a  nice surprise  good old flock but that's why it was  nice to see that line get crossed by  taking care of a major character like  Bertolt even if he is an antagonist  because he did feel like one of those  untouchable characters so it introduced  a brief period of expendability amongst  the main cast and we brought him up  briefly before but let's talk about  Arman for a bit this guy was the star of  my favorite episode of the season being  here the moment of almond sacrifice was  so for want of a better word heroic and  I don't mean that in the traditional  coming to the rescue kind of way but  didn't though this is everything I can  do and if it cost me my life and that's  fine kind of way of all of the main  characters in the season I feel like  almond stands out and received by far  the most gwyrth although to be fair I  don't think he has a lot of competition  I mean Mikasa look I honestly struggled  to remember a single thing she did this  season for a primary protagonist she  spent a lot of time in the background or  just quietly standing beside Aaron and  as for Aaron himself I've honestly never  cared for him and I wasn't about to stop  now he's just too intense for my liking  you know always yelling in  the early days noise crying and  depressed in these days that get it he's  been through unimaginable experiences  that would crumble the mind of any  average person but I get really bored  when it's just him on screen wallowing  in his own  pitov Aaron feels so I reckon just make  him a secondary protagonist and let's  promote Levi two main protagonists and  speaking of Levi it was awesome as  always this character stole attack on  Titan the moment he was introduced and  as expected he provides another  phenomenal spectacle this season with a  completely one-sided fight against the  Beast Titan and Wow I rewatched that  saying like seven times after the  episode ended it's so good and his  flashback was also done really well  because even though I knew it was going  to happen the anime still made me feel  everything I needed to which was a ton  of sympathy a little disgust and a  healthy serving of what the hell this  world is dark and that goes for the  entire series really I think it did  everything it needed to do and more to  provide a fantastic viewing experience  in an incredibly immersive world driving  ever forward towards its conclusion and  as a result for the first time in a very  long time I'm actually looking forward  to more attack on Titan so bring on  season four in 2020 if that pretty much  does it for my thoughts on sea